{
  "id": "COMPOUND:982 - DISEASE:1527",
  "verbalization": "Hydrochlorothiazide, a thiazide diuretic, appears to exert a causal relationship with Hyperparathyroidism, Secondary, through a shared pathway involving beta-1,3-galactosyltransferase 6. This enzyme is implicated in the pathogenesis of Hyperparathyroidism, Secondary, as it is a causative agent in the condition. The evidence is corroborated by multiple paths, each linking hydrochlorothiazide to a distinct intermediate entity—ankyrin repeat domain 28, deleted in azoospermia 3, and adhesion G protein-coupled receptor B3—that ultimately converge on beta-1,3-galactosyltransferase 6. These intermediates are all associated with Erythrokeratoderma en cocardes, a rare skin condition, which in turn is causally linked to Hyperparathyroidism, Secondary. The integration of these pathways suggests a potential mechanism by which hydrochlorothiazide may influence the development of this condition, though the exact mechanism remains to be fully elucidated.",
